    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    (tid, '276. A 55-year-old male with a history of chest pain now presents pain-free. ECG shows deeply inverted T waves in V2 and V3. What does this "Wellens Syndrome" indicate?', '["Benign finding in athletes", "Critical stenosis of the proximal Left Anterior Descending (LAD) artery", "Resolution of an inferior MI", "Right ventricular hypertrophy"]', 1, 'Wellens syndrome (Type B) is characterized by deeply inverted T waves in V2-V3. It indicates a critical proximal LAD stenosis and high risk for a massive anterior wall MI.'),
    (tid, '277. Which criteria are used to diagnose an acute MI in the presence of a Left Bundle Branch Block (LBBB)?', '["Jones criteria", "Sgarbossa criteria", "Wells criteria", "Duke criteria"]', 1, 'Sgarbossa criteria are used to identify STEMI in patients with LBBB or a ventricularly paced rhythm. Points are given for concordant ST elevation or excessive discordant ST elevation.'),
    (tid, '278. A patient presents with chest pain and ECG shows upsloping ST depression with tall, symmetric T waves in leads V1-V6. What is this pattern called?', '["De Winter T-waves", "Osborn waves", "Wellens waves", "Delta waves"]', 0, 'De Winter T-waves are considered a STEMI equivalent and indicate an acute proximal LAD occlusion.'),
    (tid, '279. What is the most common symptom of a patient with a "denervated" transplanted heart experiencing an MI?', '["Crushing chest pain", "Dyspnea or epigastric discomfort", "Left arm radiation", "Jaw pain"]', 1, 'Because the transplanted heart is denervated, patients typically do not experience classic angina. They often present with "silent" MIs or atypical symptoms like dyspnea or fatigue.'),
    (tid, '280. A 4-year-old child presents with 6 days of high fever, "strawberry tongue," conjunctivitis, and a rash. What cardiac complication is most concerning?', '["Aortic dissection", "Coronary artery aneurysms", "Mitral stenosis", "Atrial fibrillation"]', 1, 'Kawasaki disease is the leading cause of acquired heart disease in children in developed countries. Untreated, up to 25% of children develop coronary artery aneurysms.'),
    (tid, '281. In the treatment of acute pulmonary edema, what is the primary benefit of Non-invasive Positive Pressure Ventilation (NIPPV) like CPAP?', '["It increases heart rate", "It reduces preload and afterload while improving oxygenation and decreasing the work of breathing", "It increases systemic vascular resistance", "It treats the underlying infection"]', 1, 'NIPPV increases intrathoracic pressure, which reduces venous return (preload) and left ventricular transmural pressure (afterload), helping "push" fluid out of the alveoli.'),
    (tid, '282. A 70-year-old patient with atrial fibrillation presents with sudden onset of severe abdominal pain. Physical exam is relatively benign ("pain out of proportion to exam"). What is the likely diagnosis?', '["Appendicitis", "Acute mesenteric ischemia", "Cholecystitis", "Small bowel obstruction"]', 1, 'Embolic occlusion of the superior mesenteric artery (often from AF) leads to acute mesenteric ischemia. The classic presentation is severe pain with a soft, non-tender abdomen early in the course.'),
    (tid, '283. A patient with a history of heart failure presents with a BP of 80/40, cold extremities, and oliguria. What type of shock is this?', '["Hypovolemic", "Cardiogenic", "Septic", "Neurogenic"]', 1, 'Cardiogenic shock is characterized by systemic hypoperfusion (hypotension, cold skin, low urine output) due to primary pump failure.'),
    (tid, '284. Which electrolyte abnormality is most commonly associated with a "shortened" QT interval?', '["Hypokalemia", "Hypercalcemia", "Hyponatremia", "Hypomagnesemia"]', 1, 'Hypercalcemia shortens the ST segment, leading to a shortened QT interval.'),
    (tid, '285. A patient with Chagas disease (Trypanosoma cruzi) is at high risk for which cardiac complication?', '["Aortic stenosis", "Dilated cardiomyopathy and conduction system disease (e.g., RBBB)", "Mitral valve prolapse", "Pericarditis"]', 1, 'Chronic Chagas disease leads to a dilated cardiomyopathy, often with apical aneurysms and significant conduction disturbances.'),
    (tid, '286. What is the most common cause of acute pericarditis in the United States?', '["Bacterial infection", "Idiopathic or Viral (e.g., Coxsackievirus)", "Uremia", "Malignancy"]', 1, 'Most cases of pericarditis are presumed viral or idiopathic and are managed with NSAIDs and colchicine.'),
    (tid, '287. Which cardiac structure is most commonly injured in blunt chest trauma?', '["Left atrium", "Right ventricle", "Aorta", "Mitral valve"]', 1, 'The right ventricle is the most anterior chamber and is thus most susceptible to injury from blunt force to the sternum.'),
    (tid, '288. A patient presents with "Electrical Storm," defined as 3 or more episodes of sustained VT or VF within 24 hours. What is a key step in management?', '["Giving high-dose Epinephrine", "Sympathetic blockade (e.g., Esmolol or Propranolol) and sedation", "Discontinuing all antiarrhythmics", "Performing a stress test"]', 1, 'Electrical storm is often driven by high sympathetic tone. Beta-blockers and sedation are critical to breaking the cycle.'),
    (tid, '289. In a patient with an Left Ventricular Assist Device (LVAD), what is the most reliable way to assess perfusion in the ED?', '["Manual palpation of the radial pulse", "Mean Arterial Pressure (MAP) measured by Doppler ultrasound and a blood pressure cuff", "Standard automatic BP cuff", "Capillary refill only"]', 1, 'Because LVADs provide continuous flow, standard pulsatile BP measurements are often impossible. Doppler MAP is the gold standard.'),
    (tid, '290. What is "Pacemaker Syndrome"?', '["A bacterial infection of the leads", "Symptoms (e.g., dizziness, syncope) caused by AV dyssynchrony in a single-chamber ventricular pacemaker", "A psychological fear of pacemakers", "Lead fracture"]', 1, 'Pacemaker syndrome occurs when ventricular contraction is not preceded by coordinated atrial contraction, leading to reduced cardiac output and retrograde VA conduction.'),
    (tid, '291. A patient with an AICD (Automated Implantable Cardioverter Defibrillator) presents after receiving 5 shocks. They are currently in sinus rhythm. What is the priority?', '["Disable the AICD immediately with a magnet", "Identify the cause of the shocks (e.g., ischemia, electrolyte imbalance) and consult cardiology", "Discharge with follow-up", "Give IV Calcium"]', 1, 'Multiple shocks ("ICD storm") require a thorough search for triggers like ischemia or electrolyte disturbances. A magnet can be used to disable further shocks if they are deemed inappropriate (e.g., firing for SVT).'),
    (tid, '292. A patient develops acute, severe Mitral Regurgitation 3 days after an MI. What is the likely anatomical cause?', '["Aortic root dissection", "Papillary muscle rupture", "Ventricular septal defect", "Left atrial thrombus"]', 1, 'Papillary muscle rupture (typically the posteromedial muscle) is a catastrophic mechanical complication of MI that leads to acute severe MR.'),
    (tid, '293. Which Stanford classification of aortic dissection always requires emergent surgical repair?', '["Type A (involves ascending aorta)", "Type B (involves descending aorta only)", "Both A and B", "Neither; both are managed medically"]', 0, 'Type A dissections involve the ascending aorta and are surgical emergencies due to the risk of coronary artery occlusion, aortic valve rupture, or tamponade.'),
    (tid, '294. What is the Jones criteria used to diagnose?', '["Endocarditis", "Acute Rheumatic Fever", "Kawasaki disease", "Aortic stenosis"]', 1, 'The Jones criteria (Major: Joint involvement, O-shaped heart/Carditis, Nodules, Erythema marginatum, Sydenham chorea) are used to diagnose Acute Rheumatic Fever.'),
    (tid, '295. A patient presents with "painful" red nodules on their fingertips. What are these called in the context of endocarditis?', '["Janeway lesions", "Osler nodes", "Roth spots", "Splinter hemorrhages"]', 1, 'Osler nodes are painful, immune-complex-mediated nodules. Janeway lesions are painless and caused by septic emboli.'),
    (tid, '296. What is the classic finding on bedside echo for cardiac tamponade during diastole?', '["LV hypertrophy", "Right ventricular diastolic collapse", "Aortic root dilation", "Mitral valve prolapse"]', 1, 'In tamponade, the high pericardial pressure exceeds the low diastolic pressure of the right ventricle, causing it to collapse inward during diastole.'),
    (tid, '297. A patient presents with a large pericardial effusion. You perform a bedside ultrasound and see the "swining heart" sign. What is the corresponding ECG finding?', '["ST elevation", "Electrical alternans", "Prolonged PR interval", "U waves"]', 1, 'Electrical alternans (varying height of QRS complexes) is the electrical manifestation of the heart physically swinging in a large effusion.'),
    (tid, '298. Which medication is the preferred treatment for hypertensive emergency with aortic dissection?', '["Hydralazine", "Esmolol followed by Nitroprusside or Nicardipine", "Nifedipine", "Clonidine"]', 1, 'The goal in dissection is to reduce both heart rate and blood pressure quickly to reduce shear stress. Esmolol is ideal for its short half-life.'),
    (tid, '299. A patient with a heart transplant presents with a HR of 100 bpm. Why is this expected?', '["Ongoing rejection", "The heart is denervated and lacks vagal tone, leading to a higher resting heart rate", "Post-op pain", "Hyperthyroidism"]', 1, 'The transplanted heart lacks parasympathetic (vagal) input, so the resting heart rate is determined by the intrinsic rate of the SA node, typically around 90-110 bpm.'),
    (tid, '300. A patient presents with a wide-complex tachycardia. You are unsure if it is VT or SVT with aberrancy. How should you treat it if the patient is unstable?', '["Wait for cardiology", "Synchronized cardioversion", "Give IV Verapamil", "Give IV Adenosine"]', 1, 'Unstable wide-complex tachycardia should always be treated as VT with immediate synchronized cardioversion. "When in doubt, shock."');
